  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Energy balance, contextual', note: 'Calories in/out still governs mass (the thermodynamics lesson) — but for athletes "out" includes training, NEAT and a metabolism that adapts; sports nutrition adds TIMING and COMPOSITION to the accounting.' },
    { math: '2 · Protein: the repair budget', note: '~1.6–2.2 g/kg/day for strength athletes (the meta-analytic consensus) — the biology lesson\'s muscle-protein synthesis, funded. Distribution matters modestly (0.4 g/kg per meal, 3–4 meals: leucine threshold).' },
    { math: '3 · Carbohydrate is the performance fuel', note: 'Glycogen is the high-intensity tank (the energy lesson) — endurance and team athletes need 5–10 g/kg/day; low-carb training works for fat adaptation but evidence favours carbs for performance. Train-low/compete-high is a protocol, not a lifestyle.' },
    { math: '4 · Hydration and sodium, honestly', note: '2% body-mass loss degrades endurance performance — drink to thirst in training, weigh before/after in heat; sodium replacement matters beyond ~2 h sweating. Overhydration (hyponatraemia) is the rarer, worse error — marathons learned this the hard way.' },
