---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/intermediate/nutrition-for-performance">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Sports nutrition is the nutrition lesson\'s physiology, <strong>aimed at performance instead
    of health alone</strong>: fuel the session, repair after, hydrate through — with the
    evidence tables the supplement aisle wishes it had.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="balance, protein, carbohydrate, hydration" widget="equationStep" config={nutriCfg} />

  <h2>What works and what\'s sold</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The evidence tier list:</strong> creatine (strong), caffeine (strong, 3–6 mg/kg), nitrate/beetroot (moderate), beta-alanine (moderate for 1–4 min events), bicarbonate (effective, gut-hostile) — versus thousands of products with no evidence (the Australian Institute of Sport\'s ABCD system grades them publicly).</li>
    <li><strong>Carb periodisation:</strong> race-week loading (supercompensating glycogen — the classic marathon tactic), intra-event fuelling for >90 min (60–90 g/h mixed glucose:fructose — gut training is real), recovery windows (~24 h matter, not 30 minutes — the "anabolic window" softened in the evidence).</li>
    <li><strong>RED-S is the modern warning:</strong> Relative Energy Deficiency in Sport — chronic underfuelling degrading bone, hormones and immunity (the bone stress fractures of gymnasts and runners); the "lighter is faster" culture is the cause, and education is the cure.</li>
    <li><strong>Supplements carry contamination risk:</strong> third-party certification (Informed Sport) exists because banned-substance contamination is a documented doping-positive cause — the anti-doping lesson\'s strict liability makes purity a career decision.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> AIS Sports Supplement Framework; ACSM/AND position stands; the training and thermodynamics lessons.</p>
</TopicPage>
